      "chord_quality": "maj6",
      "function_role": "tonic-major-sixth-shared-parent-scale",
      "narrative": "Coker prescribes deriving the major sixth chord by extracting scale degrees 1-3-5-6 from the major scale of the chord root: 'Tones No. 1-3-5-6 of the C Major scale form the C Major Sixth Chord' (Chapter 1, p. 1). Because the M6 shares the major scale with the triad, M7, and M9, patterns written under a triad symbol apply equally to CM6 -- 'The use of the triad symbol in the following patterns is merely for convenience, since it would be impossible to determine exactly which chord is being used when the scale is common to all the chords mentioned' (Chapter 4, p. 16).",
