      "chord_quality": "maj7",
      "function_role": "tonic-major-seventh-shared-parent-scale",
      "narrative": "Coker prescribes deriving the major seventh chord by extracting scale degrees 1-3-5-7 from the major scale of the chord root: 'Tones #1-3-5-7 form the C Major Seventh Chord' (Chapter 1, p. 2). The M7 shares the same parent major scale with the triad, M6, and M9, so a single scale fingering covers all four qualities (Chapter 4, p. 16). Coker also prescribes the heuristic that 'in most instances, the M7 chord is likely to function as IM7 rather than IVM7' (Chapter 16, p. 82), reducing the decision burden when key context is ambiguous.",
