      "function_role": "tonic-major-ninth-shared-parent-scale",
      "narrative": "Coker prescribes deriving the major ninth chord by extracting scale degrees 1-3-5-7-9 from the major scale of the chord root: 'Tones #1-3-5-7-9 form the C Major Ninth Chord' (Chapter 1, p. 2). The ninth is the same pitch as the second scale degree -- 'the ninth, eleventh and thirteenth are also the second, fourth and sixth tones respectively, as they are the same letter names' (Chapter 1, p. 3). Because M9 shares the same major scale parent as the triad, M6, and M7, no separate scale is needed for improvisation.",
