      "function_role": "dominant-mixolydian-source",
      "narrative": "Coker prescribes extracting scale degrees 1-3-5-7 from the Mixolydian mode to form the dominant seventh chord: 'By extracting tones No. 1-3-5-7 from the B-Flat Mixolydian mode, we arrive at the notes forming the B-Flat Dominant Seventh Chord' (Chapter 9, p. 50). The student must relate each Mixolydian mode to its parent key before applying formulas or playing improvisation patterns over the chord (Chapter 9, p. 51).",
