      "function_role": "dominant-one-diminished-scale-four-chords",
      "narrative": "Coker prescribes applying a single diminished scale to four different dominant seventh chords whose roots lie within the same diminished seventh chord: 'a diminished scale on C-sharp, E, G, or A will fit and enhance (because of the color tones provided) a C7, E-flat7, F-sharp7, or A7 chord' (Chapter 27, p. 131). This efficiency principle -- one scale serves four roots -- directly follows from the diminished-fifth substitution rule linking tritone and minor-third-related dominant chords.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jerry-coker:patterns-for-jazz:symmetrical-scale-reduction/diminished-fifth-substitution",
