      "function_role": "dominant-altered-ninth-flat9-option",
      "narrative": "Coker prescribes treating the flat-9 (minor ninth) as one of two available altered ninth options on a dominant seventh chord: 'when the ninth is added to the dominant seventh chord (1-3-5-7), it can also be augmented (+9) or minor (flat-9 -- sometimes referred to as a diminished ninth)' (Chapter 25, p. 119). Dominant function is retained as long as the third is major and the seventh is minor, regardless of the ninth's quality (Chapter 25, p. 119). The flat-9 is also one of the four color tones supplied by the diminished scale one half-step above the chord root (Chapter 27, p. 130).",
